\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Recovery of the Four Major Analyzer Equations from a Single Lagrangian.} Numerical integration of the partition Lagrangian $\mathcal  {L}= \tfrac  {1}{2}\mu |\dot  {\mathbf  {x}}|^2 + \mu \dot  {\mathbf  {x}}\cdot \mathbf  {A}_{\mathcal  {M}}- \mathcal  {M}$ on the substrate model reproduces TOF, Orbitrap, FT-ICR, and quadrupole equations of motion to within numerical tolerance. (\textbf  {A}) Time-of-flight trajectories $z(t)$ for partition inertia $\mu \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16\}$ under linear field $\mathcal  {M}(z) = -\kappa z$ (viridis colour scale: light = small $\mu $, dark = large $\mu $). Larger $\mu $ produces slower acceleration; arrival time scales as $T \propto \sqrt  {\mu }$, with relative error $5.6 \times 10^{-4}$ from the closed form $T = \sqrt  {2\mu L/\kappa }$. (\textbf  {B}) Orbitrap observed angular frequency $\omega $ versus predicted $\omega = \sqrt  {\kappa /\mu }$ for three masses, with identity line (dashed). All three points fall on the line; mean relative error $4.3 \times 10^{-7}$. (\textbf  {C}) FT-ICR cyclotron orbit in the $xy$ plane, integrated by Boris pusher with $\omega _c = B/\mu = 1$. The orbit closes onto itself with speed coefficient of variation $3.8 \times 10^{-13}$ and guiding-centre radius CV $5.5 \times 10^{-10}$; the cross marks the analytical guiding centre at $(0, -1)$. (\textbf  {D}) Mathieu equation trajectories at $a = 0$, $q = 0.3$ (green, stable, $|x|_{\max } = 1.36$) and $q = 1.5$ (red, unstable, $|x|_{\max } = 2.2 \times 10^{14}$, vertical axis symmetric log). The two regimes are distinguishable by 14 orders of magnitude, recovering the standard Mathieu first-stability boundary.}}{7}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:e1_analyzer_recovery}{{2}{7}{\textbf {Recovery of the Four Major Analyzer Equations from a Single Lagrangian.} Numerical integration of the partition Lagrangian $\Lagr = \tfrac {1}{2}\partinert |\dot {\mathbf {x}}|^2 + \partinert \dot {\mathbf {x}}\cdot \Apartition - \Depth $ on the substrate model reproduces TOF, Orbitrap, FT-ICR, and quadrupole equations of motion to within numerical tolerance. (\textbf {A}) Time-of-flight trajectories $z(t)$ for partition inertia $\partinert \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16\}$ under linear field $\Depth (z) = -\kappa z$ (viridis colour scale: light = small $\partinert $, dark = large $\partinert $). Larger $\partinert $ produces slower acceleration; arrival time scales as $T \propto \sqrt {\partinert }$, with relative error $5.6 \times 10^{-4}$ from the closed form $T = \sqrt {2\partinert L/\kappa }$. (\textbf {B}) Orbitrap observed angular frequency $\omega $ versus predicted $\omega = \sqrt {\kappa /\partinert }$ for three masses, with identity line (dashed). All three points fall on the line; mean relative error $4.3 \times 10^{-7}$. (\textbf {C}) FT-ICR cyclotron orbit in the $xy$ plane, integrated by Boris pusher with $\omega _c = B/\partinert = 1$. The orbit closes onto itself with speed coefficient of variation $3.8 \times 10^{-13}$ and guiding-centre radius CV $5.5 \times 10^{-10}$; the cross marks the analytical guiding centre at $(0, -1)$. (\textbf {D}) Mathieu equation trajectories at $a = 0$, $q = 0.3$ (green, stable, $|x|_{\max } = 1.36$) and $q = 1.5$ (red, unstable, $|x|_{\max } = 2.2 \times 10^{14}$, vertical axis symmetric log). The two regimes are distinguishable by 14 orders of magnitude, recovering the standard Mathieu first-stability boundary}{figure.2}{}}
